    "path": "cross_encoder/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "author = \"Reimers, Nils and Gurevych, Iryna\",\nbooktitle = \"Proceedings of the 2020 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing\",\nmonth = \"11\",\nyear = \"2020\",\npublisher = \"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\nurl = \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09813\",\n}\nPlease have a look at Publications for our different publications that are integrated into SentenceTransformers.\nMaintainer: Tom Aarsen, 🤗 Hugging Face\nDon't hesitate to open an issue if something is broken (and it shouldn't be) or if you have further questions.\nThis project was originally developed by the Ubiquitous Knowledge Processing (UKP) Lab at TU Darmstadt. We're grateful for their foundational work and continued contributions to the field.\nThis repository contains experimental software and is published for the sole purpose of giving additional background details on the respective publication.",
    "character_count": 885
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1a911ab5-eeb5-4ffc-a394-856bc3a64312",
    "technology": "Cross-Encoder",
    "technology_id": "source-cross-encoder",
    "category": "rerankers",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://sbert.net/examples/cross_encoder/applications/README.html",
    "path": "cross_encoder/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Cross-Encoders\nSentenceTransformers also supports to load Cross-Encoders for sentence pair scoring and sentence pair classification tasks.\nCross-Encoder vs. Bi-Encoder\nFirst, it is important to understand the difference between Bi- and Cross-Encoder.\nBi-Encoders produce for a given sentence a sentence embedding. We pass to a BERT independently the sentences A and B, which result in the sentence embeddings u and v. These sentence embedding can then be compared using cosine similarity:\nIn contrast, for a Cross-Encoder, we pass both sentences simultaneously to the Transformer network. It produces then an output value between 0 and 1 indicating the similarity of the input sentence pair:\nA Cross-Encoder does not produce a sentence embedding. Also, we are not able to pass individual sentences to a Cross-Encoder.",
    "character_count": 817
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "802058a2-f128-4b61-9389-fa9465bb35b4",
    "technology": "Cross-Encoder",
    "technology_id": "source-cross-encoder",
    "category": "rerankers",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://sbert.net/examples/cross_encoder/applications/README.html",
    "path": "cross_encoder/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "A Cross-Encoder does not produce a sentence embedding. Also, we are not able to pass individual sentences to a Cross-Encoder.\nAs detailed in our paper, Cross-Encoder achieve better performances than Bi-Encoders. However, for many application they are not practical as they do not produce embeddings we could e.g. index or efficiently compare using cosine similarity.\nWhen to use Cross- / Bi-Encoders?\nCross-Encoders can be used whenever you have a pre-defined set of sentence pairs you want to score. For example, you have 100 sentence pairs and you want to get similarity scores for these 100 pairs.",
    "character_count": 600
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c4eeb4f7-0d3b-4352-ba89-7592152b9871",
    "technology": "Cross-Encoder",
    "technology_id": "source-cross-encoder",
    "category": "rerankers",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://sbert.net/examples/cross_encoder/applications/README.html",
    "path": "cross_encoder/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Cross-Encoders can be used whenever you have a pre-defined set of sentence pairs you want to score. For example, you have 100 sentence pairs and you want to get similarity scores for these 100 pairs.\nBi-Encoders (see Computing Sentence Embeddings) are used whenever you need a sentence embedding in a vector space for efficient comparison. Applications are for example Information Retrieval / Semantic Search or Clustering. Cross-Encoders would be the wrong choice for these application: Clustering 10,000 sentence with CrossEncoders would require computing similarity scores for about 50 Million sentence combinations, which takes about 65 hours. With a Bi-Encoder, you compute the embedding for each sentence, which takes only 5 seconds. You can then perform the clustering.\nCross-Encoders Usage\nUsing Cross-Encoders is quite easy:\nfrom sentence_transformers.cross_encoder import CrossEncoder\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L6-v2\")",
    "character_count": 954
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6a4092d2-1c1e-430e-96ab-f944462196dd",
    "technology": "Cross-Encoder",
    "technology_id": "source-cross-encoder",
    "category": "rerankers",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://sbert.net/examples/cross_encoder/applications/README.html",
    "path": "cross_encoder/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "Cross-Encoders Usage\nUsing Cross-Encoders is quite easy:\nfrom sentence_transformers.cross_encoder import CrossEncoder\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\nscores = model.predict([[\"My first\", \"sentence pair\"], [\"Second text\", \"pair\"]])\nYou pass to model.predict a list of sentence pairs. Note, Cross-Encoder do not work on individual sentence, you have to pass sentence pairs.\nAs model name, you can pass any model or path that is compatible with Hugging Face AutoModel class.\nFor a full example, to score a query with all possible sentences in a corpus see cross_encoder_usage.py.\nCombining Bi- and Cross-Encoders",
    "character_count": 637
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "23016a49-aff3-4def-ba03-a4f90281581f",
    "technology": "Cross-Encoder",
    "technology_id": "source-cross-encoder",
    "category": "rerankers",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://sbert.net/examples/cross_encoder/applications/README.html",
    "path": "cross_encoder/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "For a full example, to score a query with all possible sentences in a corpus see cross_encoder_usage.py.\nCombining Bi- and Cross-Encoders\nCross-Encoder achieve higher performance than Bi-Encoders, however, they do not scale well for large datasets. Here, it can make sense to combine Cross- and Bi-Encoders, for example in Information Retrieval / Semantic Search scenarios: First, you use an efficient Bi-Encoder to retrieve e.g. the top-100 most similar sentences for a query. Then, you use a Cross-Encoder to re-rank these 100 hits by computing the score for every (query, hit) combination.\nFor more details on combing Bi- and Cross-Encoders, see Application - Information Retrieval.\nTraining Cross-Encoders\nSee Cross-Encoder Training how to train your own Cross-Encoder models.",
    "character_count": 780
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b488c240-c23b-46a4-840e-79608d793028",
    "technology": "DSPy",
    "technology_id": "source-dspy",
    "category": "prompting",
