  {
    "chunk_id": "fd57c138-485b-4e69-a61a-a94a7e586297",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "-\nNotifications\nYou must be signed in to change notification settings\n- Fork 4.5k\nIndexing 1M vectors\nThis page gives some advice and results on datasets of around 1M vectors.\nWhen the dataset is around 1m vectors, the exhaustive index becomes too slow, so a good alternative is IndexIVFFlat. It still returns exact distances but occasionally misses a neighbor because it is non-exhaustive.\nExperiments from 2018\nBelow are a few experiments on datasets of size 1M with different indexing methods. We focus on the tradeoff between:\n- speed, measured on a \"Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU E5-2680 v2 @ 2.80GHz\" with 20 threads enabled. It is reported in ms in batch mode (ie. all query vectors are handled simultaneously).\n- accuracy. We use the //1-NN recall @R// measure with R=1, 10, or 100. This is the fraction of query vectors where the actual nearest-neighbor is ranked in the R first results.",
    "character_count": 887
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d6f39f80-c92f-40d6-8bb3-25221e0a4fee",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- accuracy. We use the //1-NN recall @R// measure with R=1, 10, or 100. This is the fraction of query vectors where the actual nearest-neighbor is ranked in the R first results.\nWARNING: This is an optimistic setting, running with a single thread is about 10x slower and providing queries one by one is another 2-3x slower (see the timings for sift_1M below)\nThe index types that are tested are referred to by their index key, as created by the index_factory.\nFor all indexes, we request 100 results per query.\nWe use 4096D descriptors extracted from 1M images, and the same from query images. The 4096D descriptors are then reduced to 256D by PCA (beforehand, the cost of the PCA transformation is not reflected in the results). The most appropriate indexes for this are:\nSo an IVF is better for high accuracy regimes, and IMI for lower regimes.",
    "character_count": 846
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f07231c2-e8f6-4a0f-83c9-45f157f1fe2d",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "So an IVF is better for high accuracy regimes, and IMI for lower regimes.\nNOTE: IVF16384 gives slightly better results than IVF4096, but the later is significantly cheaper to do train and add's (this cost is not reflected in the measurements).\nIf memory is a concern, then compressed vectors can be used (with IndexIVFPQ)\nIVF4096,Flat is plotted only for reference.\nFor example: OPQ32_128,IVF4096,PQ32 uses size of the code + id = 32 + 8 = 40 bytes per vector. This makes is total of 40MB total for the database, but there are overheads due to precomputed tables, geometric array allocation, etc.\nWe also use resnet-34 descriptors trained on Imagenet and computed on the 1M first images of Flickr100M, from which we remove the two upper layers to get 512D activation maps. The behavior is similar:\nThis is a common benchmark used in research papers. It consists of SIFT descriptors (128D) extracted from image patches.\nThe comments are about the same as for the CNN data.",
    "character_count": 971
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4378f57c-635a-4847-96d6-bcbb9c43d998",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "This is a common benchmark used in research papers. It consists of SIFT descriptors (128D) extracted from image patches.\nThe comments are about the same as for the CNN data.\nHere we also evaluated the performance in relation with threading and batching:\n-\nnt1: batched, single-thread\n-\nnobatch/nobatch_nt1: queries are submitted 1 by 1\n-\nparallelqueries: queries are submitted in parallel 1 by 1, and processed by a non-batch loop.\nIt appears that a 1-thread run is 5x to 12x slower. To submit queries one at a time, threading is not useful.\nNOTE: in this plot we report the speed in QPS (queries per second) and the x-axis is the 10-precision at 10 measure (or intersection measure). This is for easy comparison with nmslib, which is the best library on this benchmark.",
    "character_count": 770
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "275dbcdb-a307-46d1-88d7-18de5ffff32a",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "A comparison with the benchmarks above is not accurate because the machines are not the same. A direct comparison with nmslib shows that nmslib is faster, but uses significantly more memory. For Faiss, the build time is sub-linear and memory usage is linear.\n| | search time | 1-R@1 | index size | index build time |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Flat-CPU | 9.100 s | 1.0000 | 512 MB | 0 s |\n| nmslib (hnsw) | 0.081 s | 0.8195 | 512 + 796 MB | 173 s |\n| IVF16384,Flat | 0.538 s | 0.8980 | 512 + 8 MB | 240 s |\n| IVF16384,Flat (Titan X) | 0.059 s | 0.8145 | 512 + 8 MB | 5 s |\n| Flat-GPU (Titan X) | 0.753 s | 0.9935 | 512 MB | 0 s |\nThe first row is for exact search with Faiss. The two last results are with a GPU (Titan X). The Flat indexes are brute force indexes that return exact results (up to ties and floating-point precision issues).\nThis is used as a benchmark by Annoy. The performance measure is different: intersection of the found 10-NN with the GT 10-NN.",
    "character_count": 963
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9d0042e1-e8b2-4b2d-8000-e4c0e9622726",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "This is used as a benchmark by Annoy. The performance measure is different: intersection of the found 10-NN with the GT 10-NN.\nThe IVFPQ followed by a verification improves the results slightly.\nWe use data sampled uniformly on a unit sphere in 128D. This is the hardest data to index because there is no regularity whatsoever that the indexing method can exploit.\nThe measured performance is recall @ 100, otherwise it would be too low. Here, for most operating points, doing a brute-force computation is the best we can do.\nThere are several uses of HNSW as an indexing method in FAISS:\n-\nthe normal HNSW that operates on full vectors\n-\noperate on quantized vectors (SQ)\n-\nas a quantizer for an IVF\n-\nas an assignment index for kmeans\nThe various use cases are evaluated with benchs/bench_hnsw.py on SIFT1M. The output looks like (with 20 threads):\ntesting HNSW Flat\nefSearch 16 0.011 ms per query, R@1 0.8740\nefSearch 32 0.020 ms per query, R@1 0.9492\nefSearch 64 0.033 ms per query, R@1 0.9779",
    "character_count": 997
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6e5784d3-e959-46cc-aeae-21d6157b270a",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "testing HNSW Flat\nefSearch 16 0.011 ms per query, R@1 0.8740\nefSearch 32 0.020 ms per query, R@1 0.9492\nefSearch 64 0.033 ms per query, R@1 0.9779\nefSearch 128 0.059 ms per query, R@1 0.9887\nefSearch 256 0.104 ms per query, R@1 0.9920\ntesting HNSW with a scalar quantizer\nefSearch 16 0.005 ms per query, R@1 0.7281\nefSearch 32 0.008 ms per query, R@1 0.8506\nefSearch 64 0.011 ms per query, R@1 0.9242\nefSearch 128 0.020 ms per query, R@1 0.9566\nefSearch 256 0.039 ms per query, R@1 0.9716\ntesting IVF Flat (baseline)\nnprobe 1 0.076 ms per query, R@1 0.4085\nnprobe 4 0.067 ms per query, R@1 0.6331\nnprobe 16 0.078 ms per query, R@1 0.8263\nnprobe 64 0.141 ms per query, R@1 0.9470\nnprobe 256 0.344 ms per query, R@1 0.9861\ntesting IVF Flat with HNSW quantizer\nnprobe 1 0.007 ms per query, R@1 0.4058\nnprobe 4 0.010 ms per query, R@1 0.6305\nnprobe 16 0.021 ms per query, R@1 0.8247\nnprobe 64 0.063 ms per query, R@1 0.9462\nnprobe 256 0.220 ms per query, R@1 0.9842\nThe comparisons show:\n-",
    "character_count": 985
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "86791de1-a7d5-46da-b9b6-e2cd7e911c54",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "nprobe 4 0.010 ms per query, R@1 0.6305\nnprobe 16 0.021 ms per query, R@1 0.8247\nnprobe 64 0.063 ms per query, R@1 0.9462\nnprobe 256 0.220 ms per query, R@1 0.9842\nThe comparisons show:\n-\nHNSW obtains much better speed / precision operating points than IVFFlat (eg. 0.020 ms vs. 0.140 ms to get > 0.9 recall at 1), at a higher memory cost\n-\nHNSW with a scalar quantizer is better than the classical HNSW (note that that SIFT1M is originally encoded in bytes)\n-\nusing HNSW as a quantizer on top of a memory-efficient IVF improves the search speed (the performance is similar to an IMI quantizer, but clusters are more balanced)\nTests on kmeans clustering:\nperforming kmeans on the sift1M vectors (baseline)\nClustering 1000000 points in 128D to 16384 clusters, redo 1 times, 10 iterations\nPreprocessing in 0.17 s\nIteration 9 (612.53 s, search 612.18 s): objective=3.85228e+10 imbalance=1.235 nsplit=0\nperforming kmeans on the sift1M using HNSW assignment",
    "character_count": 952
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1276fe4e-a536-4939-a810-9d237880d2e8",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "Preprocessing in 0.17 s\nIteration 9 (612.53 s, search 612.18 s): objective=3.85228e+10 imbalance=1.235 nsplit=0\nperforming kmeans on the sift1M using HNSW assignment\nClustering 1000000 points in 128D to 16384 clusters, redo 1 times, 10 iterations\nPreprocessing in 0.17 s\nIteration 9 (74.63 s, search 73.46 s): objective=3.85232e+10 imbalance=1.234 nsplit=0\nie. the clustering is 8.2x times faster than with an exhaustive assignment, without impacting the objective value or increasing the number of empty clusters (nsplit) or imbalance factor, both are signs of unhealthy clusters.\nThere is an efficient 4-bit PQ implementation in Faiss. We compare the Faiss fast-scan implementation with Google's SCANN, version 1.1.1. The 4-bit PQ implementation of Faiss is heavily inspired by SCANN. The data layout is tuned to be efficient with AVX instructions, see simulate_kernels_PQ4.ipynb.\ndetails",
    "character_count": 890
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6844c1fa-fdf3-4fd3-8a10-ff975a68db4a",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "details\nHere we set the number of search threads to 1. For all benchmarks we report plots of an accuracy measure (x-axis) vs. the number of queries per second (QPS, y-axis): up and right is better. All experiments were performed on a normalized platform (2.2 GHz Xeon E5-2698, 80 cores).\nWe use four 1M-sized datasets:\n-\nSIFT1M: classical dataset of SIFT vectors in 128 dim\n-\nDeep1M: dataset of 96-dim L2 normalized deep features\n-\nglove-100: the dataset used in ANN-benchmarks, comparison in cosine distance ( faiss.INNER_PRODUCT ).\n-\nmusic-100: a dataset for maximum inner product search introduced in Morozov & Babenko, \"Non-metric similarity graphs for maximum inner product search.\" NIPS'18.\nWe used the recommended settings from SCANN on all datasets:\nin Faiss terms it means a factory string of IVF2000,PQdim/2x4fs,RFlat (we test with and without the RFlat reranking step).",
    "character_count": 880
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4b35f60b-7cf8-4e20-bec7-61207eae9066",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "We used the recommended settings from SCANN on all datasets:\nin Faiss terms it means a factory string of IVF2000,PQdim/2x4fs,RFlat (we test with and without the RFlat reranking step).\nWe get 10 results per query vector. When re-ranking (RFlat), we instead search 100 vectors and rerank those with exact distance computations.\nHere Faiss is faster and more accurate than SCANN for all operating points before re-ranking, and for most operating points after re-ranking.\nSame conclusion as for SIFT1M.\nNote that here we use the original accuracy measure used in ANN benchmarks (inter @ 10) instead of the 1-recall@10 in the other plots.",
    "character_count": 633
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "bb962dd5-aedd-42f7-9be8-0a31a22cf39c",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "text": "Same conclusion as for SIFT1M.\nNote that here we use the original accuracy measure used in ANN benchmarks (inter @ 10) instead of the 1-recall@10 in the other plots.\nGlove has a specific data distribution due to the way the vectors are trained. SCANN includes an anisotropic quantization (see Accelerating Large-Scale Inference with Anisotropic Vector Quantization, Guo et al, ICML'20) technique that improves the accuracy in this case. However, after re-ranking the difference is not so strong anymore, and Faiss is even better on some operating points. Therefore, we do not consider it urgent to add an implementation of anisotropic quantization to Faiss.\nThe improvement of Faiss over SCANN is larger for Music-100 than for the L2 datasets. This shows that the anisotropic quantization used in SCANN is not useful for all maximum inner product use cases.\nThis experiment shows that the Faiss implementation of is on-par and often better than the SCANN implementation",
    "character_count": 969
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f55863ea-9e8e-44c8-b0f0-524710793f1a",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 12,
    "text": "This experiment shows that the Faiss implementation of is on-par and often better than the SCANN implementation\nNote that in reality, one would use multiple threads and add a refinement stage, which we do in the following experiments.\nFor all benchmarks we report plots of an accuracy measure (x-axis) vs. the number of queries per second (QPS, y-axis): up and right is better. All experiments were performed on a normalized platform (2.2 GHz Xeon E5-2698, 80 cores). We did perform all experiments with 32 corse, which is a relatively common setup.\nThe search depends on parameters, for example the nprobe parameter that indicates the number of quantizer cells to visit per query. To set them, we use the auto-tuning functionality of Faiss that find the set of Pareto-optimal search parameters in an index.\nWe start with a small exploration: on SIFT1M we compare the fast-scan version of PQ with the widely used PQ8 and scalar quantizers. The dataset is first split in an IVF1024.",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7e0559cb-b30d-4d39-9f38-b54a5a577e93",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Indexing-1M-vectors",
    "path": "faiss/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 13,
    "text": "We start with a small exploration: on SIFT1M we compare the fast-scan version of PQ with the widely used PQ8 and scalar quantizers. The dataset is first split in an IVF1024.\nThe results are below:\nIVF1024 -- encodings\nThe labels of each plot also report the number of bytes per stored vector. This does not matter much for 1M-sized datasets but at a larger scale it\nObservations:\n-\nfor the low-accuracy regime, the fast-scan PQ4 implementation offers the best tradeoff\n-\nfor the high-accuracy regime, HNSW has the best ones.\n-\nthe PQ64x4fs plot has limited accuracy topline, it is even lower than the PQ64x4 without fs because the later uses a residual encoding and float32 look-up tables (it is of course much slower)\n-\nthe PQ64x4fsr is slightly slower than PQ64x4fs but has a bit higher topline accuracy. This is because it encodes residual vectors. For larger vocabularies, the impact of the residual vectors is stronger.",
    "character_count": 924
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "39d610a4-cbfb-4a35-98f1-58612b974552",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
