    "path": "faiss/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Welcome to Faiss Documentation\nFaiss\nFaiss is a library for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It contains algorithms that search in sets of vectors of any size, up to ones that possibly do not fit in RAM. It also contains supporting code for evaluation and parameter tuning.\nFaiss is written in C++ with complete wrappers for Python. Some of the most useful algorithms are implemented on the GPU. It is developed primarily at FAIR, the fundamental AI research team of Meta.\nWhat is similarity search?\nGiven a set of vectors \\(x_i\\) in dimension \\(d\\), Faiss builds a data structure in RAM from it. After the structure is constructed, when given a new vector \\(x\\) in dimension \\(d\\) it performs efficiently the operation:\nwhere \\(\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert\\) is the Euclidean distance (\\(L^2\\)).\nIn Faiss terms, the data structure is an index, an object that has an add method to add \\(x_i\\) vectors. Note that the \\(x_i\\)’s are assumed to be fixed.",
    "character_count": 967
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8182e52b-c3dd-4b46-8572-bcb8fafbb7e9",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://faiss.ai/",
    "path": "faiss/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "In Faiss terms, the data structure is an index, an object that has an add method to add \\(x_i\\) vectors. Note that the \\(x_i\\)’s are assumed to be fixed.\nComputing the argmin is the search operation on the index.\nThis is all what Faiss is about. It can also:\n- return not just the nearest neighbor, but also the 2nd nearest, 3rd, …, k-th nearest neighbor\n- search several vectors at a time rather than one (batch processing). For many index types, this is faster than searching one vector after another\n- trade precision for speed, ie. give an incorrect result 10% of the time with a method that’s 10x faster or uses 10x less memory\n- perform maximum inner product search \\(argmax_i \\langle x, x_i \\rangle\\) instead of minimum Euclidean search. There is also limited support for other distances (L1, Linf, etc.).\n- return all elements that are within a given radius of the query point (range search)\n- store the index on disk rather than in RAM.",
    "character_count": 945
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0d7bf55f-5659-422e-bc78-32609a8fa4c9",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://faiss.ai/",
    "path": "faiss/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "- return all elements that are within a given radius of the query point (range search)\n- store the index on disk rather than in RAM.\n- index binary vectors rather than floating-point vectors\n- ignore a subset of index vectors according to a predicate on the vector ids.\nInstall\nThe recommended way to install Faiss is through Conda:\n$ conda install -c pytorch faiss-cpu\nThe faiss-gpu package provides CUDA-enabled indices:\n$ conda install -c pytorch faiss-gpu\nNote that either package should be installed, but not both, as the latter is a superset of the former.\nResearch foundations of Faiss\nFaiss is based on years of research. Most notably it implements:\n- The inverted file from “Video google: A text retrieval approach to object matching in videos.”, Sivic & Zisserman, ICCV 2003. This is the key to non-exhaustive search in large datasets. Otherwise all searches would need to scan all elements in the index, which is prohibitive even if the operation to apply for each element is fast",
    "character_count": 991
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "79ca3384-6f64-4f35-af40-c7999344db3f",
    "technology": "FAISS",
    "technology_id": "source-faiss",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Meta AI",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://faiss.ai/",
    "path": "faiss/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "- The product quantization (PQ) method from “Product quantization for nearest neighbor search”, Jégou & al., PAMI 2011. This can be seen as a lossy compression technique for high-dimensional vectors, that allows relatively accurate reconstructions and distance computations in the compressed domain.\n- The three-level quantization (IVFADC-R aka IndexIVFPQR) method from “Searching in one billion vectors: re-rank with source coding”, Tavenard & al., ICASSP’11.\n- The inverted multi-index from “The inverted multi-index”, Babenko & Lempitsky, CVPR 2012. This method greatly improves the speed of inverted indexing for fast/less accurate operating points.\n- The optimized PQ from “Optimized product quantization”, He & al, CVPR 2013. This method can be seen as a linear transformation of the vector space to make it more amenable for indexing with a product quantizer.",
    "character_count": 866
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f44a2953-30e1-4470-8921-cae263794668",
