    "path": "graph_of_thoughts_got/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "This is the official implementation of Graph of Thoughts: Solving Elaborate Problems with Large Language Models.\nThis framework gives you the ability to solve complex problems by modeling them as a Graph of Operations (GoO), which is automatically executed with a Large Language Model (LLM) as the engine.\nThis framework is designed to be flexible and extensible, allowing you to not only solve problems using the new GoT approach, but also to implement GoOs resembling previous approaches like CoT or ToT.\nIn order to use this framework, you need to have a working installation of Python 3.8 or newer.\nBefore running either of the following two installation methods, make sure to activate your Python environment (if any) beforehand.\nIf you are a user and you just want to use graph_of_thoughts, you can install it directly from PyPI:\npip install graph_of_thoughts\nIf you are a developer and you want to modify the code, you can install it in editable mode from source:",
    "character_count": 970
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c80da8ed-98be-4d74-954c-04b01341de02",
    "technology": "Graph of Thoughts (GoT)",
    "technology_id": "source-graph-of-thoughts",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "Independent Research",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/spcl/graph-of-thoughts",
    "path": "graph_of_thoughts_got/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "pip install graph_of_thoughts\nIf you are a developer and you want to modify the code, you can install it in editable mode from source:\ngit clone https://github.com/spcl/graph-of-thoughts.git\ncd graph-of-thoughts\npip install -e .\nIn order to use the framework, you need to have access to an LLM. Please follow the instructions in the Controller README to configure the LLM of your choice.\nThe following code snippet shows how to use the framework to solve the sorting problem for a list of 32 numbers using a CoT-like approach.\nMake sure you have followed the Setup Guide before running the code.\nfrom examples.sorting.sorting_032 import SortingPrompter, SortingParser, utils\nfrom graph_of_thoughts import controller, language_models, operations\n# Problem input\nto_be_sorted = \"[0, 2, 6, 3, 8, 7, 1, 1, 6, 7, 7, 7, 7, 9, 3, 0, 1, 7, 9, 1, 3, 5, 1, 3, 6, 4, 5, 4, 7, 3, 5, 7]\"\n# Create the Graph of Operations\ngop = operations.GraphOfOperations()\ngop.append_operation(operations.Generate())",
    "character_count": 988
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1975ade3-a3f7-44ea-86c9-f2d400b8a002",
    "technology": "Graph of Thoughts (GoT)",
    "technology_id": "source-graph-of-thoughts",
