    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16130",
    "path": "graphrag/research_papers.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Abstract:The use of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to retrieve relevant information from an external knowledge source enables large language models (LLMs) to answer questions over private and/or previously unseen document collections. However, RAG fails on global questions directed at an entire text corpus, such as \"What are the main themes in the dataset?\", since this is inherently a query-focused summarization (QFS) task, rather than an explicit retrieval task. Prior QFS methods, meanwhile, do not scale to the quantities of text indexed by typical RAG systems. To combine the strengths of these contrasting methods, we propose GraphRAG, a graph-based approach to question answering over private text corpora that scales with both the generality of user questions and the quantity of source text",
    "character_count": 808
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "17b04f75-e88b-41b3-8601-39d00f27e509",
