    "path": "k3s/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "GitHub Issues - Submit your issues and feature requests via GitHub.\nThe K3s developer community hangs out on Zoom to chat. Everybody is welcome.\nAdd the Linux Foundation iCal to your calendar:\n- AMS/EMEA TZ 10:00 am PST - every second Tuesday of the month\n- EMEA/APAC TimeZone friendly - every third Tuesday of the month\nMeeting notes and agenda: https://hackmd.io/@k3s/meet-notes/\nMeeting recordings: K3s Channel\nYou can check also the full details on the website: https://k3s.io/community\nCheck out our roadmap to see what we have planned moving forward.\nK3s maintains pace with upstream Kubernetes releases. Our goal is to release patch releases within one week, and new minors within 30 days.",
    "character_count": 696
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d7c1f1f5-f9b2-4f19-bb31-20d7975fda75",
    "technology": "K3s",
    "technology_id": "source-k3s",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Rancher",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/k3s-io/k3s",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "K3s maintains pace with upstream Kubernetes releases. Our goal is to release patch releases within one week, and new minors within 30 days.\nOur release versioning reflects the version of upstream Kubernetes that is being released. For example, the K3s release v1.27.4+k3s1 maps to the v1.27.4 Kubernetes release. We add a postfix in the form of +k3s<number> to allow us to make additional releases using the same version of upstream Kubernetes while remaining semver compliant. For example, if we discovered a high severity bug in v1.27.4+k3s1 and needed to release an immediate fix for it, we would release v1.27.4+k3s2.\nPlease see the official docs site for complete documentation.\nThe install.sh script provides a convenient way to download K3s and add a service to systemd or openrc.\nTo install k3s as a service, run:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -\nA kubeconfig file is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml and the service is automatically started or restarted.",
    "character_count": 970
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "78186e79-b4a2-407d-b6c3-80a6e3e0e677",
    "technology": "K3s",
    "technology_id": "source-k3s",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Rancher",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/k3s-io/k3s",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "To install k3s as a service, run:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -\nA kubeconfig file is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml and the service is automatically started or restarted.\nThe install script will install K3s and additional utilities, such as kubectl, crictl, k3s-killall.sh, and k3s-uninstall.sh, for example:\nsudo kubectl get nodes\nK3S_TOKEN is created at /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token on your server.\nTo install on worker nodes, pass K3S_URL along with\nK3S_TOKEN environment variables, for example:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | K3S_URL=https://myserver:6443 K3S_TOKEN=XXX sh -\n- Download k3s from latest release, x86_64, armhf, arm64 and s390x are supported.\n- Run the server.\nsudo k3s server &\n# Kubeconfig is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml\nsudo k3s kubectl get nodes\n# On a different node run the below. NODE_TOKEN comes from\n# /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token on your server\nsudo k3s agent --server https://myserver:6443 --token ${NODE_TOKEN}",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ca32b485-47a6-446f-b7cb-ed77e1f46728",
    "technology": "K3s",
    "technology_id": "source-k3s",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Rancher",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
