  {
    "chunk_id": "a643e940-9582-44fb-860a-7ec85dfdc2e7",
    "technology": "LM Studio",
    "technology_id": "source-lm-studio",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "LM Studio",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://lmstudio.ai/docs",
    "path": "lm_studio/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Welcome to LM Studio Docs!\nLearn how to run Llama, DeepSeek, Qwen, Phi, and other LLMs locally with LM Studio.\nExplore the docs\nTo get LM Studio, head over to the Downloads page and download an installer for your operating system.\nLM Studio is available for macOS, Windows, and Linux.\nWhat can I do with LM Studio?\n- Download and run local LLMs like gpt-oss or Llama, Qwen\n- Use a simple and flexible chat interface\n- Connect MCP servers and use them with local models\n- Search & download functionality (via Hugging Face 🤗)\n- Serve local models on OpenAI-like endpoints, locally and on the network\n- Manage your local models, prompts, and configurations\nSystem requirements\nLM Studio generally supports Apple Silicon Macs, x64/ARM64 Windows PCs, and x64 Linux PCs.\nConsult the System Requirements page for more detailed information.\nRun llama.cpp (GGUF) or MLX models\nLM Studio supports running LLMs on Mac, Windows, and Linux using llama.cpp.",
    "character_count": 943
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a5584f7f-2850-435d-b029-d04207d6b5e5",
    "technology": "LM Studio",
    "technology_id": "source-lm-studio",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "LM Studio",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://lmstudio.ai/docs",
    "path": "lm_studio/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Consult the System Requirements page for more detailed information.\nRun llama.cpp (GGUF) or MLX models\nLM Studio supports running LLMs on Mac, Windows, and Linux using llama.cpp.\nOn Apple Silicon Macs, LM Studio also supports running LLMs using Apple's MLX.\nTo install or manage LM Runtimes, press ⌘ Shift R on Mac or Ctrl Shift R on Windows/Linux.\nLM Studio as an MCP client\nYou can install MCP servers in LM Studio and use them with your local models.\nSee the docs for more: Use MCP server.\nIf you're develping an MCP server, check out Add to LM Studio Button.\nRun an LLM like gpt-oss, Llama, Qwen, Mistral, or DeepSeek R1 on your computer\nTo run an LLM on your computer you first need to download the model weights.\nYou can do this right within LM Studio! See Download an LLM for guidance.\nChat with documents entirely offline on your computer\nYou can attach documents to your chat messages and interact with them entirely offline, also known as \"RAG\".",
    "character_count": 955
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a8e1fd66-7daf-4af7-a330-167c0cd8d57d",
    "technology": "LM Studio",
    "technology_id": "source-lm-studio",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "LM Studio",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://lmstudio.ai/docs",
    "path": "lm_studio/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Chat with documents entirely offline on your computer\nYou can attach documents to your chat messages and interact with them entirely offline, also known as \"RAG\".\nRead more about how to use this feature in the Chat with Documents guide.\nRun LM Studio without the GUI (llmster)\nllmster is the headless version of LM Studio, no desktop app required. It's ideal for servers, CI environments, or any machine where you don't need a GUI.\nLearn more: Headless Mode.\nUse LM Studio's API from your own apps and scripts\nLM Studio provides a REST API that you can use to interact with your local models from your own apps and scripts.\nCommunity\nJoin the LM Studio community on Discord to ask questions, share knowledge, and get help from other users and the LM Studio team.",
    "character_count": 762
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2a705220-e7c9-4416-8810-400cba2cfe4d",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "A programming language for large language models.\n\nDocumentation »\n\n\nExplore Examples\n·\nPlayground IDE\n·\nReport Bug\n\n\n\n\n\nLMQL is a programming language for large language models (LLMs) based on a superset of Python. LMQL offers a novel way of interweaving traditional programming with the ability to call LLMs in your code. It goes beyond traditional templating languages by integrating LLM interaction natively at the level of your program code.",
    "character_count": 446
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6c296544-d7c4-466a-b0a1-404bb4fb8aa1",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Help us shape the next major version of LMQL by filling out the LMQL developer survey: https://forms.gle/pGvAicNpUhS1rAkK9\nAn LMQL program reads like standard Python, but top-level strings are interpreted as query strings: They are passed to an LLM, where template variables like [GREETINGS] are automatically completed by the model:\n\"Greet LMQL:[GREETINGS]\\n\" where stops_at(GREETINGS, \".\") and not \"\\n\" in GREETINGS\nif \"Hi there\" in GREETINGS:\n\"Can you reformulate your greeting in the speech of \\\nvictorian-era English: [VIC_GREETINGS]\\n\" where stops_at(VIC_GREETINGS, \".\")\n\"Analyse what part of this response makes it typically victorian:\\n\"\nfor i in range(4):\n\"-[THOUGHT]\\n\" where stops_at(THOUGHT, \".\")\n\"To summarize:[SUMMARY]\"\nProgram Output:",
    "character_count": 749
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f3c0d22b-1099-4a88-9f6d-96fc652914c0",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "\"Analyse what part of this response makes it typically victorian:\\n\"\nfor i in range(4):\n\"-[THOUGHT]\\n\" where stops_at(THOUGHT, \".\")\n\"To summarize:[SUMMARY]\"\nProgram Output:\nLMQL allows you to express programs that contain both, traditional algorithmic logic, and LLM calls. At any point during execution, you can prompt an LLM on program variables in combination with standard natural language prompting, to leverage model reasoning capabilities in the context of your program.\nTo better control LLM behavior, you can use the where keyword to specify constraints and data types of the generated text. This enables guidance of the model's reasoning process, and constraining of intermediate outputs using an expressive constraint language.\nBeyond this linear form of scripting, LMQL also supports a number of decoding algorithms to execute your program, such as argmax, sample or even advanced branching decoders like beam search and best_k.",
    "character_count": 940
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "72b57a67-e836-43a4-bb48-eba67cbef82a",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "Learn more about LMQL by exploring thne Example Showcase, by running your own programs in our browser-based Playground IDE or by reading the documentation.\nLMQL is designed to make working with language models like OpenAI and 🤗 Transformers more efficient and powerful through its advanced functionality, including multi-variable templates, conditional distributions, constraints, datatypes and control flow.\n- Python Syntax: Write your queries using familiar Python syntax, fully integrated with your Python environment (classes, variable captures, etc.)\n- Rich Control-Flow: LMQL offers full Python support, enabling powerful control flow and logic in your prompting logic.\n- Advanced Decoding: Take advantage of advanced decoding techniques like beam search, best_k, and more.\n- Powerful Constraints Via Logit Masking: Apply constraints to model output, e.g. to specify token length, character-level constraints, datatype and stopping phrases to get more control of model behavior.",
    "character_count": 984
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "935f7365-98ab-4c2e-9582-e4602d3d4f0d",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "- Optimizing Runtime: LMQL leverages speculative execution to enable faster inference, constraint short-circuiting, more efficient token use and tree-based caching.\n- Sync and Async API: Execute hundreds of queries in parallel with LMQL's asynchronous API, which enables cross-query batching.\n- Multi-Model Support: Seamlessly use LMQL with OpenAI API, Azure OpenAI, and 🤗 Transformers models.\n- Extensive Applications: Use LMQL to implement advanced applications like schema-safe JSON decoding, algorithmic prompting, interactive chat interfaces, and inline tool use.\n- Library Integration: Easily employ LMQL in your existing stack leveraging LangChain or LlamaIndex.\n- Flexible Tooling: Enjoy an interactive development experience with LMQL's Interactive Playground IDE, and Visual Studio Code Extension.\n- Output Streaming: Stream model output easily via WebSocket, REST endpoint, or Server-Sent Event streaming.",
    "character_count": 916
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7e1fcbfa-3ece-460f-83e1-d6b654399f21",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "- Output Streaming: Stream model output easily via WebSocket, REST endpoint, or Server-Sent Event streaming.\nTo install the latest version of LMQL run the following command with Python ==3.10 installed.\npip install lmql\nLocal GPU Support: If you want to run models on a local GPU, make sure to install LMQL in an environment with a GPU-enabled installation of PyTorch >= 1.11 (cf. https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/) and install via pip install lmql[hf].\nAfter installation, you can launch the LMQL playground IDE with the following command:\nlmql playground\nUsing the LMQL playground requires an installation of Node.js. If you are in a conda-managed environment you can install node.js via conda install nodejs=14.20 -c conda-forge. Otherwise, please see the official Node.js website https://nodejs.org/en/download/ for instructions how to install it on your system.",
    "character_count": 873
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "05e35620-6bd2-4f9b-9191-994d414c98e9",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "This launches a browser-based playground IDE, including a showcase of many exemplary LMQL programs. If the IDE does not launch automatically, go to http://localhost:3000.\nAlternatively, lmql run can be used to execute local .lmql files. Note that when using local HuggingFace Transformers models in the Playground IDE or via lmql run, you have to first launch an instance of the LMQL Inference API for the corresponding model via the command lmql serve-model.\nIf you want to use OpenAI models, you have to configure your API credentials. To do so you can either define the OPENAI_API_KEY environment variable or create a file api.env in the active working directory, with the following contents:\nopenai-org: <org identifier>\nopenai-secret: <api secret>\nFor system-wide configuration, you can also create an api.env file at $HOME/.lmql/api.env or at the project root of your LMQL distribution (e.g. src/ in a development copy).",
    "character_count": 926
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "152d46fe-c347-49f9-ad7f-fa810d85b8b7",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "For system-wide configuration, you can also create an api.env file at $HOME/.lmql/api.env or at the project root of your LMQL distribution (e.g. src/ in a development copy).\nAlternatively, you can use LMQL-specific env variables LMQL_OPENAI_SECRET and LMQL_OPENAI_ORG.\nTo install the latest (bleeding-edge) version of LMQL, you can also run the following command:\npip install git+https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql\nThis will install the lmql package directly from the main branch of this repository. We do not continously test the main version, so it may be less stable than the latest PyPI release.\nLMQL is a community-centric project. If you are interested in contributing to LMQL, please see the contributing guidelines for more information, and reach out to us via Discord. We are looking forward to your contributions!\nTo setup a conda environment for local LMQL development with GPU support, run the following commands:\n# prepare conda environment",
    "character_count": 950
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e890b8eb-848f-4cd1-96e3-519d36dc0abb",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "To setup a conda environment for local LMQL development with GPU support, run the following commands:\n# prepare conda environment\nconda env create -f scripts/conda/requirements.yml -n lmql\nconda activate lmql\n# registers the `lmql` command in the current shell\nsource scripts/activate-dev.sh\nOperating System: The GPU-enabled version of LMQL was tested to work on Ubuntu 22.04 with CUDA 12.0 and Windows 10 via WSL2 and CUDA 11.7. The no-GPU version (see below) was tested to work on Ubuntu 22.04 and macOS 13.2 Ventura or Windows 10 via WSL2.\nThis section outlines how to setup an LMQL development environment without local GPU support. Note that LMQL without local GPU support only supports the use of API-integrated models like openai/text-davinci-003. Please see the OpenAI API documentation (https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-3-5) to learn more about the set of available models.\nTo setup a conda environment for LMQL with no GPU support, run the following commands:",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4ab12490-863c-4442-ae8d-0125bd98811c",
    "technology": "LMQL",
    "technology_id": "source-lmql",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "ETH Zurich",
    "license": "MIT",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/eth-sri/lmql",
    "path": "lmql/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "To setup a conda environment for LMQL with no GPU support, run the following commands:\n# prepare conda environment\nconda env create -f scripts/conda/requirements-no-gpu.yml -n lmql-no-gpu\nconda activate lmql-no-gpu\n# registers the `lmql` command in the current shell\nsource scripts/activate-dev.sh",
    "character_count": 297
  },
  {
