    "source": "api_reference",
    "url": "https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference",
    "path": "openai_gpt/api_reference.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "API Overview\nUse this reference to look up OpenAI API endpoints, request and response schemas, streaming events, client library methods, and shared behavior such as authentication, errors, rate limits, and request IDs.\nStart here\nSection titled “Start here”\n- Choose the API surface for your application:\n- Responses for direct model requests, tool use, audio, image, and text inputs, and stateful interactions.\n- Realtime API for low-latency voice or audio sessions over WebRTC, WebSocket, or SIP. Use the client events and server events references while building sessions.\n- Administration for organization workflows such as users, invites, projects, API keys, and audit logs.\n- Create credentials. Use a standard API key for application requests, an Admin API key for Administration endpoints, or workload identity federation for short-lived access tokens.",
    "character_count": 859
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "09dbaaf1-93d2-4e8b-9d1e-0e343554f44e",
    "technology": "OpenAI GPT",
    "technology_id": "source-openai-gpt",
    "category": "llms",
    "organization": "OpenAI",
    "license": "Proprietary",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "api_reference",
    "url": "https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference",
    "path": "openai_gpt/api_reference.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- Create credentials. Use a standard API key for application requests, an Admin API key for Administration endpoints, or workload identity federation for short-lived access tokens.\n- Install an official client library from the libraries page, or call the HTTP API directly from any environment that supports HTTP requests.\n- Make a first request with the developer quickstart or go straight to the Responses create reference.\n- Before production, review error codes, rate limits, and request ID logging below.\nAuthentication\nSection titled “Authentication”\nThe OpenAI API accepts bearer credentials from API keys or from short-lived access tokens created with workload identity federation.\nRemember that your API key is a secret. Don’t share it with others or expose it in any client-side code such as browsers or apps. Load API keys from an environment variable or key management service on the server.",
    "character_count": 903
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "391675b1-cb9a-4174-83dd-e4a23efaf371",
    "technology": "OpenAI GPT",
    "technology_id": "source-openai-gpt",
    "category": "llms",
    "organization": "OpenAI",
    "license": "Proprietary",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "api_reference",
    "url": "https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference",
    "path": "openai_gpt/api_reference.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Revocations of an API key take effect within a few seconds. Most updates that affect authentication results of an API key propagate within 15 minutes, but can potentially take longer.\nProvide API credentials with HTTP Bearer authentication.\nAuthorization: Bearer OPENAI_API_KEY_OR_ACCESS_TOKEN\nIf you belong to more than one organization or access projects through a legacy user API key, pass a header to specify which organization and project to use for an API request:\ncurl https://api.openai.com/v1/models \\\n-H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY\" \\\n-H \"OpenAI-Organization: $ORGANIZATION_ID\" \\\n-H \"OpenAI-Project: $PROJECT_ID\"\nUsage from these API requests counts as usage for the specified organization and project. Find organization and project IDs in your dashboard settings.\nDebugging requests\nSection titled “Debugging requests”",
    "character_count": 840
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2350203f-95f7-4bc4-a835-a86b63ee1755",
