    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/huggingface/peft",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "| Submission Name | Accuracy |\n|---|---|\n| Human baseline (crowdsourced) | 0.897 |\n| Flan-T5 | 0.892 |\n| lora-t0-3b | 0.863 |\nTip\nThe bigscience/T0_3B model performance isn't optimized in the table above. You can squeeze even more performance out of it by playing around with the input instruction templates, LoRA hyperparameters, and other training related hyperparameters. The final checkpoint size of this model is just 19MB compared to 11GB of the full bigscience/T0_3B model. Learn more about the advantages of finetuning with PEFT in this blog post.\nQuantization is another method for reducing the memory requirements of a model by representing the data in a lower precision. It can be combined with PEFT methods to make it even easier to train and load LLMs for inference.\n- Learn how to finetune meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf with QLoRA and the TRL library on a 16GB GPU in the Finetune LLMs on your own consumer hardware using tools from PyTorch and Hugging Face ecosystem blog post.",
    "character_count": 987
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4859d3e6-d0d4-4471-9a1f-76ed244ca26d",
    "technology": "PEFT",
    "technology_id": "source-peft",
    "category": "fine_tuning",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/huggingface/peft",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "- Learn how to finetune a openai/whisper-large-v2 model for multilingual automatic speech recognition with LoRA and 8-bit quantization in this notebook (see this notebook instead for an example of streaming a dataset).\nPEFT can help you save storage by avoiding full finetuning of models on each of downstream task or dataset. In many cases, you're only finetuning a very small fraction of a model's parameters and each checkpoint is only a few MBs in size (instead of GBs). These smaller PEFT adapters demonstrate performance comparable to a fully finetuned model. If you have many datasets, you can save a lot of storage with a PEFT model and not have to worry about catastrophic forgetting or overfitting the backbone or base model.\nPEFT is widely supported across the Hugging Face ecosystem because of the massive efficiency it brings to training and inference.",
    "character_count": 865
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fa921d67-a558-41e0-ad8f-bfd7036dbf03",
    "technology": "PEFT",
    "technology_id": "source-peft",
    "category": "fine_tuning",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/huggingface/peft",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "PEFT is widely supported across the Hugging Face ecosystem because of the massive efficiency it brings to training and inference.\nThe iterative diffusion process consumes a lot of memory which can make it difficult to train. PEFT can help reduce the memory requirements and reduce the storage size of the final model checkpoint. For example, consider the memory required for training a Stable Diffusion model with LoRA on an A100 80GB GPU with more than 64GB of CPU RAM. The final model checkpoint size is only 8.8MB!\n| Model | Full Finetuning | PEFT-LoRA | PEFT-LoRA with Gradient Checkpointing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CompVis/stable-diffusion-v1-4 | 27.5GB GPU / 3.97GB CPU | 15.5GB GPU / 3.84GB CPU | 8.12GB GPU / 3.77GB CPU |\nTip",
    "character_count": 731
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b23b8f57-f83b-4612-a8de-3c292c956d32",
    "technology": "PEFT",
    "technology_id": "source-peft",
    "category": "fine_tuning",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/huggingface/peft",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "|---|---|---|---|\n| CompVis/stable-diffusion-v1-4 | 27.5GB GPU / 3.97GB CPU | 15.5GB GPU / 3.84GB CPU | 8.12GB GPU / 3.77GB CPU |\nTip\nTake a look at the examples/lora_dreambooth/train_dreambooth.py training script to try training your own Stable Diffusion model with LoRA, and play around with the smangrul/peft-lora-sd-dreambooth Space which is running on a T4 instance. Learn more about the PEFT integration in Diffusers in this tutorial.\nPEFT is directly integrated with Transformers. After loading a model, call add_adapter to add a new PEFT adapter to the model:\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nmodel = ... # transformers model\npeft_config = LoraConfig(...)\nmodel.add_adapter(peft_config, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")\nTo load a trained PEFT adapter, call load_adapter:\nmodel = ... # transformers model\nmodel.load_adapter(<path-to-adapter>, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")\nAnd to switch between different adapters, call set_adapter:\nmodel.set_adapter(\"lora_2\")",
    "character_count": 945
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "22e8f4ac-38bb-44db-a22e-153dda4240aa",
    "technology": "PEFT",
    "technology_id": "source-peft",
    "category": "fine_tuning",
    "organization": "Hugging Face",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/huggingface/peft",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "model = ... # transformers model\nmodel.load_adapter(<path-to-adapter>, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")\nAnd to switch between different adapters, call set_adapter:\nmodel.set_adapter(\"lora_2\")\nThe Transformers integration doesn't include all the functionalities offered in PEFT, such as methods for merging the adapter into the base model.\nAccelerate is a library for distributed training and inference on various training setups and hardware (GPUs, TPUs, Apple Silicon, etc.). PEFT models work with Accelerate out of the box, making it really convenient to train really large models or use them for inference on consumer hardware with limited resources.\nPEFT can also be applied to training LLMs with RLHF components such as the ranker and policy. Get started by reading:\n- Fine-tune a Mistral-7b model with Direct Preference Optimization with PEFT and the TRL library to learn more about the Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) method and how to apply it to a LLM.",
    "character_count": 956
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "91b50fb0-3f3e-429d-a556-3440b71390c5",
    "technology": "PEFT",
    "technology_id": "source-peft",
