  {
    "chunk_id": "10648d3e-7b31-4554-af5b-c75e1de63b00",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "api_reference",
    "url": "https://api.qdrant.tech/",
    "path": "qdrant/api_reference.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "API Reference\nQdrant is a vector database and a semantic search engine. You can use its REST API to develop a production-ready service to store, search, and manage vectors with an additional payload.\nHow does Qdrant work?\n- First, you should create a collection to store all your data.\n- Then upsert data points and enrich them with a custom payload.\n- With a full collection, run a search to find relevant results.\n- Collections can be snapshotted, downloaded and restored.\n- When ready, setup a distributed system for production.\nJust getting started?\nTry the development quickstart guide.",
    "character_count": 591
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a743b509-e856-4fa3-bbd2-e43b2ee4be81",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Benchmarks F.A.Q.\nAre we biased?\nProbably, yes. Even if we try to be objective, we are not experts in using all the existing vector search engines.\nWe build Qdrant and know the most about it.\nDue to that, we could have missed some important tweaks in different vector search engines.\nHowever, we tried our best, kept scrolling the docs up and down, experimented with combinations of different configurations, and gave all of them an equal chance to stand out. If you believe you can do it better than us, our benchmarks are fully open-sourced, and contributions are welcome!\nWhat do we measure?\nThere are several factors considered while deciding on which database to use.\nOf course, some of them support a different subset of functionalities, and those might be a key factor to make the decision.\nBut in general, we all care about the search precision, speed, and resources required to achieve it.",
    "character_count": 898
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6f25fddb-c167-4106-80e5-313e221596d7",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "But in general, we all care about the search precision, speed, and resources required to achieve it.\nThere is one important thing - the speed of the vector search engines should to be compared only if they achieve the same precision. Otherwise, they could maximize the speed factors by providing inaccurate results, which everybody would rather avoid. Thus, our benchmark results are compared only at a specific search precision threshold.\nHow we select hardware?\nIn our experiments, we are not focusing on the absolute values of the metrics but rather on a relative comparison of different engines.\nWhat is important is the fact we used the same machine for all the tests.\nIt was just wiped off between launching different engines.\nWe selected an average machine, which you can easily rent from almost any cloud provider. No extra quota or custom configuration is required.\nWhy you are not comparing with FAISS or Annoy?",
    "character_count": 921
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "5c764a62-5311-4c5e-96dd-960eacd85705",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "We selected an average machine, which you can easily rent from almost any cloud provider. No extra quota or custom configuration is required.\nWhy you are not comparing with FAISS or Annoy?\nLibraries like FAISS provide a great tool to do experiments with vector search. But they are far away from real usage in production environments.\nIf you are using FAISS in production, in the best case, you never need to update it in real-time. In the worst case, you have to create your custom wrapper around it to support CRUD, high availability, horizontal scalability, concurrent access, and so on.\nSome vector search engines even use FAISS under the hood, but a search engine is much more than just an indexing algorithm.\nWe do, however, use the same benchmark datasets as the famous ann-benchmarks project, so you can align your expectations for any practical reasons.\nWhy we decided to test with the Python client",
    "character_count": 908
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8c61d95e-af55-4b67-935f-b255fdb84729",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "We do, however, use the same benchmark datasets as the famous ann-benchmarks project, so you can align your expectations for any practical reasons.\nWhy we decided to test with the Python client\nThere is no consensus when it comes to the best technology to run benchmarks. You’re free to choose Go, Java or Rust-based systems. But there are two main reasons for us to use Python for this:\n- While generating embeddings you’re most likely going to use Python and python based ML frameworks.\n- Based on GitHub stars, python clients are one of the most popular clients across all the engines.\nFrom the user’s perspective, the crucial thing is the latency perceived while using a specific library - in most cases a Python client.\nNobody can and even should redefine the whole technology stack, just because of using a specific search tool.\nThat’s why we decided to focus primarily on official Python libraries, provided by the database authors.",
    "character_count": 939
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "465abd4e-1e1a-4f0e-ab9d-a4889846c249",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "That’s why we decided to focus primarily on official Python libraries, provided by the database authors.\nThose may use some different protocols under the hood, but at the end of the day, we do not care how the data is transferred, as long as it ends up in the target location.\nThere are some vector search engines available as SaaS only so that we couldn’t test them on the same machine as the rest of the systems.\nThat makes the comparison unfair. That’s why we purely focused on testing the Open Source vector search engines, so everybody may reproduce the benchmarks easily.\nThis is not the final list, and we’ll continue benchmarking as many different engines as possible.\nHow to reproduce the benchmark?\nThe source code is available on Github and has a README.md file describing the process of running the benchmark for a specific engine.\nHow to contribute?",
    "character_count": 862
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "888d3f54-bc39-4fb9-94bf-04d261c5347a",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "benchmark_pages",
    "url": "https://qdrant.tech/benchmarks/",
    "path": "qdrant/benchmark_pages.json",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "How to reproduce the benchmark?\nThe source code is available on Github and has a README.md file describing the process of running the benchmark for a specific engine.\nHow to contribute?\nWe made the benchmark Open Source because we believe that it has to be transparent. We could have misconfigured one of the engines or just done it inefficiently. If you feel like you could help us out, check out our benchmark repository.",
    "character_count": 423
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "72e09671-37cc-4835-9bf4-60835396bc0a",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Vector Search Engine for the next generation of AI applications\nQdrant (read: quadrant) is a vector similarity search engine and vector database. It provides a production-ready service with a convenient API to store, search, and manage points—vectors with an additional payload. Qdrant is tailored for extended filtering support, making it useful for all sorts of neural-network or semantic-based matching, faceted search, and other applications.\nQdrant is written in Rust 🦀, which makes it fast and reliable even under high load. See benchmarks.\nWith Qdrant, embeddings or neural network encoders can be turned into full-fledged applications for matching, searching, recommending, and much more!\nQdrant is also available as a fully managed Qdrant Cloud ⛅ including a free tier.\nQuick Start • Agent Skills • Client Libraries • Demo Projects • Integrations • Contact",
    "character_count": 865
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d39346fd-ec08-4842-a69b-acd07229da83",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Qdrant is also available as a fully managed Qdrant Cloud ⛅ including a free tier.\nQuick Start • Agent Skills • Client Libraries • Demo Projects • Integrations • Contact\nQdrant provides a collection of ready-to-use agent skills that bring Qdrant's vector search capabilities directly into your AI coding assistant. Install these skills to empower your agent in making critical engineering decisions for optimal vector search performance, such as quantization, sharding, tenant isolation, hybrid search, model migration, and more.\nTo experience the full power of Qdrant locally, run the container with this command:\ndocker run -p 6333:6333 qdrant/qdrant\nNote that this starts an insecure deployment without authentication, open to all network interfaces. Please refer to secure your instance.\nNow you can connect to the server with any client. For example, using Python:\nfrom qdrant_client import QdrantClient\nclient = QdrantClient(url=\"http://localhost:6333\")",
    "character_count": 958
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c5b7c89e-fe2a-4ea9-80f4-52ea429b33fa",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Now you can connect to the server with any client. For example, using Python:\nfrom qdrant_client import QdrantClient\nclient = QdrantClient(url=\"http://localhost:6333\")\nBefore deploying Qdrant to production, be sure to read our installation and security guides.\nQdrant offers the following client libraries to help you integrate it into your application stack:\n- Official:\n- Community:\nQdrant Edge is a lightweight version of Qdrant designed for edge devices and resource-constrained environments. Unlike Qdrant Server, which uses a client-server architecture, Qdrant Edge runs inside the application process. Data is stored and queried locally and can be synchronized with a Qdrant server. It offers the same powerful vector search capabilities as the client-server version but with a smaller footprint, making it ideal for applications that require low latency and offline functionality.",
    "character_count": 888
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2682efb7-771b-41d3-8eaf-d7af55dbee88",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "To get started with Qdrant Edge from Python or Rust, initialize an instance of EdgeShard, which exposes methods to manage data, query it, and restore snapshots. For example:\nfrom qdrant_edge import Distance, EdgeConfig, EdgeVectorParams, EdgeShard, Point, UpdateOperation\nshard = EdgeShard.create(\"./shard\", EdgeConfig(\nvectors={\"my-vector\": EdgeVectorParams(size=4, distance=Distance.Cosine)}\n))\nshard.update(UpdateOperation.upsert_points([\nPoint(id=1, vector={\"my-vector\": [0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4]}, payload={\"color\": \"red\"})\n]))\n- Quick Start Guide\n- Detailed Documentation\n- Take the Qdrant Essentials course\n- Follow this tutorial to create a semantic search engine with Qdrant\nUnlock the power of semantic embeddings with Qdrant, transcending keyword-based search to find meaningful connections in short texts. Deploy a neural search in minutes using a pre-trained neural network, and experience the future of text search. Try it online!",
    "character_count": 939
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e36c4426-8463-49fd-b7a2-a5255b87775f",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "There's more to discovery than text search, especially when it comes to food. People often choose meals based on appearance rather than descriptions and ingredients. Let Qdrant help your users find their next delicious meal using visual search, even if they don't know the dish's name. Check it out!\nEnter the cutting-edge realm of extreme classification, an emerging machine learning field tackling multi-class and multi-label problems with millions of labels. Harness the potential of similarity learning models, and see how a pre-trained transformer model and Qdrant can revolutionize e-commerce product categorization. Play with it online!\nQdrant provides a REST API with an OpenAPI 3.0 specification, enabling client generation for virtually any framework or programming language.\nYou can also download the raw OpenAPI definitions.\nFor faster, production-tier searches, Qdrant also provides a gRPC interface.",
    "character_count": 913
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2d0b0e06-1763-4bbc-b5a7-b33f77e978d1",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "You can also download the raw OpenAPI definitions.\nFor faster, production-tier searches, Qdrant also provides a gRPC interface.\nQdrant supports dense vectors for semantic similarity, sparse vectors for full-text search, and multivector search for objects with multiple embeddings or late interaction models like ColBERT.\nAttach any JSON payload to your vectors and filter on it using a rich set of conditions—keyword matching, full-text, numeric ranges, geo-locations, and more—combined with should, must, and must_not clauses.\nCombine multiple vectors in a single query to get the best of semantic understanding and keyword precision, with results merged via configurable fusion strategies, such as Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) and Distribution-Based Score Fusion (DBSF).\nBuilt-in quantization cuts RAM usage by up to 97% and lets you tune the trade-off between search speed and precision.\nScale horizontally with sharding and replication, and update or resize collections with zero downtime.",
    "character_count": 993
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "bb774b59-29dd-4ea3-9b4a-d3edd7ef6b92",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "Scale horizontally with sharding and replication, and update or resize collections with zero downtime.\n- Faceting - aggregate search results by payload values.\n- Recommendation - use positive and negative examples to find similar points.\n- Discovery - constrain search to a specific region of the vector space.\n- Search Relevance Tuning - tools for adjusting search results, such as Maximal Marginal Relevance (MMR) and the Relevance Feedback Query.\n- Multitenancy - scalable partitioning of data for multi-user environments.\n- Observability - comprehensive metrics, telemetry, and audit logging for monitoring and debugging.\n- Query Planning and Payload Indexes - leverages stored payload information to optimize query execution strategy.\n- SIMD Hardware Acceleration - utilizes modern CPU x86-x64 and Neon architectures to deliver better performance.\n- GPU Support - for accelerated indexing, with support for NVIDIA and AMD GPUs.",
    "character_count": 932
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "200797bc-9c72-4da5-b18f-cb6509ef3cc9",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant",
    "path": "qdrant/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "- SIMD Hardware Acceleration - utilizes modern CPU x86-x64 and Neon architectures to deliver better performance.\n- GPU Support - for accelerated indexing, with support for NVIDIA and AMD GPUs.\n- Async I/O - uses io_uring to maximize disk throughput utilization even on network-attached storage.\n- Write-Ahead Logging - ensures data persistence with update confirmation, even during power outages.\nWeb UI provides a visual way to interact with your data and monitor the health of your deployment. It enables you to explore your collections, manage data, interact with the REST API, and more.\nQdrant integrates with the tools you're already using across every stage of your AI stack. You can connect to embedding providers, AI application frameworks, and data pipeline tools, as well as observability platforms for monitoring and tracing your vector search in production. No-code and low-code automation platforms are supported too. Refer to the Ecosystem page for the complete list.",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4c7a33a8-ccf4-4b78-a93c-93800bba5003",
    "technology": "Qdrant",
    "technology_id": "source-qdrant",
    "category": "vectordbs",
    "organization": "Qdrant Solutions",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
