    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Computer Science > Computation and Language\n[Submitted on 21 Mar 2022 (v1), last revised 7 Mar 2023 (this version, v4)]\nTitle:Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models\nView PDF",
    "character_count": 206
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d4fe845a-94dc-460e-b81c-42682500dabe",
    "technology": "Self-Consistency",
    "technology_id": "source-self-consistency",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "Independent Research",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171",
    "path": "self_consistency/research_papers.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "View PDF\nAbstract:Chain-of-thought prompting combined with pre-trained large language models has achieved encouraging results on complex reasoning tasks. In this paper, we propose a new decoding strategy, self-consistency, to replace the naive greedy decoding used in chain-of-thought prompting. It first samples a diverse set of reasoning paths instead of only taking the greedy one, and then selects the most consistent answer by marginalizing out the sampled reasoning paths. Self-consistency leverages the intuition that a complex reasoning problem typically admits multiple different ways of thinking leading to its unique correct answer. Our extensive empirical evaluation shows that self-consistency boosts the performance of chain-of-thought prompting with a striking margin on a range of popular arithmetic and commonsense reasoning benchmarks, including GSM8K (+17.9%), SVAMP (+11.0%), AQuA (+12.2%), StrategyQA (+6.4%) and ARC-challenge (+3.9%).",
    "character_count": 956
  },
