    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Computer Science > Computation and Language\n[Submitted on 27 Aug 2019]\nTitle:Sentence-BERT: Sentence Embeddings using Siamese BERT-Networks\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\nAbstract:BERT (Devlin et al., 2018) and RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019) has set a new state-of-the-art performance on sentence-pair regression tasks like semantic textual similarity (STS). However, it requires that both sentences are fed into the network, which causes a massive computational overhead: Finding the most similar pair in a collection of 10,000 sentences requires about 50 million inference computations (~65 hours) with BERT. The construction of BERT makes it unsuitable for semantic similarity search as well as for unsupervised tasks like clustering.",
    "character_count": 731
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1f846bf5-df27-4d60-8a66-4e2c240edd1a",
    "technology": "Sentence Transformers",
    "technology_id": "source-sentence-transformers",
    "category": "embeddings",
    "organization": "UKPLab",
    "license": "Apache 2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10084",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/research_papers.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "In this publication, we present Sentence-BERT (SBERT), a modification of the pretrained BERT network that use siamese and triplet network structures to derive semantically meaningful sentence embeddings that can be compared using cosine-similarity. This reduces the effort for finding the most similar pair from 65 hours with BERT / RoBERTa to about 5 seconds with SBERT, while maintaining the accuracy from BERT.\nWe evaluate SBERT and SRoBERTa on common STS tasks and transfer learning tasks, where it outperforms other state-of-the-art sentence embeddings methods.\nReferences & Citations",
    "character_count": 589
  },
