    "path": "vercel_edge_functions/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Vercel Functions\nexport default {\nfetch(request: Request) {\nreturn new Response('Hello from Vercel!');\n},\n};\nWhen you deploy your application, Vercel automatically sets up the tools and optimizations for your chosen framework. It ensures low latency by routing traffic through Vercel's CDN, and placing your functions in a specific region when you need more control over data locality.\n- Zero server management. Deploy code that scales automatically with traffic\n- Fluid compute. Reduced cold starts, lower latency, and lower costs via optimized concurrency\n- Multi-runtime support. Use Node.js, Python, Go, and more\n- Regional control. Pin functions to a region close to your data source\n- Framework-aware. Vercel detects and optimizes for your framework automatically\n- Built-in observability. View invocations, errors, and cost metrics in the dashboard\nCopy the code below to create your first function:\nexport default {\nfetch(request: Request) {\nreturn new Response('Hello from Vercel!');\n},\n};",
    "character_count": 998
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9e7aa67f-344a-4539-8555-cb9239ae4d13",
    "technology": "Vercel Edge Functions",
    "technology_id": "source-vercel-edge-functions",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Vercel",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://vercel.com/docs/functions",
    "path": "vercel_edge_functions/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Copy the code below to create your first function:\nexport default {\nfetch(request: Request) {\nreturn new Response('Hello from Vercel!');\n},\n};\nWhile using fetch is the recommended way to create a Vercel Function, you can still use HTTP methods like GET and POST.\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\nreturn new Response('Hello from Vercel!');\n}\nTo learn more, see the quickstart or deploy a template.\nVercel Functions handle each incoming request as a new invocation, scaling automatically so you don't need to manage servers. They run in a single region by default, although you can configure them to run in multiple regions if you have globally replicated data. These functions let you add extra capabilities to your application, such as handling authentication, streaming data, or querying databases.\nWhen a user sends a request to your site, Vercel can automatically run a function based on your application code. You don't need to manage servers or handle scaling.",
    "character_count": 972
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "80fc3f40-56da-4448-9a40-e82d531a9eda",
    "technology": "Vercel Edge Functions",
    "technology_id": "source-vercel-edge-functions",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Vercel",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://vercel.com/docs/functions",
    "path": "vercel_edge_functions/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "When a user sends a request to your site, Vercel can automatically run a function based on your application code. You don't need to manage servers or handle scaling.\nVercel creates a new function invocation for each incoming request. If another request arrives soon after the previous one, Vercel reuses the same function instance to optimize performance and cost efficiency. Over time, Vercel only keeps as many active functions as needed to handle your traffic. Vercel scales your functions down to zero when there are no incoming requests.\nBy allowing concurrent execution within the same instance (and so using idle time for compute), fluid compute reduces cold starts, lowers latency, and saves on compute costs. It also prevents the need to spin up multiple isolated instances when tasks spend most of their time waiting for external operations.",
    "character_count": 851
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "032c900b-a08e-4e6d-94fd-1d444dd3360f",
    "technology": "Vercel Edge Functions",
    "technology_id": "source-vercel-edge-functions",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "Vercel",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "url": "https://vercel.com/docs/functions",
    "path": "vercel_edge_functions/official_documentation.json",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "Functions should always execute close to where your data source is to reduce latency. By default, functions using the Node.js runtime execute in Washington, D.C., USA (iad1), a common location for external data sources. You can set a new region through your project's settings on Vercel.\nYou can view various performance and cost efficiency metrics using Vercel Observability:\n- Choose your project from the dashboard.\n- Click on the Observability tab and select the Vercel Functions section.\n- Click on the chevron icon to expand and see all charts.\nYou'll see current function usage metrics, including Active CPU, Provisioned Memory, Invocations, and savings from the optimized concurrency model when fluid compute is enabled.\nVercel Functions are priced based on active CPU, provisioned memory, and invocations. See the fluid compute pricing documentation for more information.\nIf your project is not using fluid compute, see the legacy pricing documentation for Vercel Functions.",
    "character_count": 983
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "504eff56-e2b0-42ab-86c6-f952be3f9b45",
    "technology": "Vercel Edge Functions",
