    "path": "zero_shot_prompting/research_papers.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Computer Science > Computation and Language\n[Submitted on 3 Sep 2021 (v1), last revised 8 Feb 2022 (this version, v5)]\nTitle:Finetuned Language Models Are Zero-Shot Learners\nView PDF\nAbstract:This paper explores a simple method for improving the zero-shot learning abilities of language models. We show that instruction tuning -- finetuning language models on a collection of tasks described via instructions -- substantially improves zero-shot performance on unseen tasks.",
    "character_count": 473
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9913d493-ca9b-45c1-bbde-0f77bc69fdc1",
    "technology": "Zero-Shot Prompting",
    "technology_id": "source-zero-shot-prompting",
    "category": "prompting",
    "organization": "Independent Research",
    "license": "",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.01652",
    "path": "zero_shot_prompting/research_papers.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "We take a 137B parameter pretrained language model and instruction-tune it on over 60 NLP tasks verbalized via natural language instruction templates. We evaluate this instruction-tuned model, which we call FLAN, on unseen task types. FLAN substantially improves the performance of its unmodified counterpart and surpasses zero-shot 175B GPT-3 on 20 of 25 tasks that we evaluate. FLAN even outperforms few-shot GPT-3 by a large margin on ANLI, RTE, BoolQ, AI2-ARC, OpenbookQA, and StoryCloze. Ablation studies reveal that number of finetuning datasets, model scale, and natural language instructions are key to the success of instruction tuning.\nSubmission history\nFrom: Jason Wei [view email]\n[v1] Fri, 3 Sep 2021 17:55:52 UTC (488 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 5 Oct 2021 02:13:12 UTC (850 KB)\n[v3] Thu, 4 Nov 2021 23:05:34 UTC (850 KB)\n[v4] Thu, 2 Dec 2021 00:45:29 UTC (999 KB)\n[v5] Tue, 8 Feb 2022 20:26:45 UTC (1,230 KB)\nReferences & Citations",
    "character_count": 933
  },
