  {
    "chunk_id": "2f6ed8d6-b02b-46bd-befe-e990503609e8",
    "technology": "vLLM",
    "technology_id": "source-vllm",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "vLLM",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm",
    "path": "vllm/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "| Documentation | Blog | Paper | Twitter/X | User Forum | Developer Slack |\n🔥 We have built a vLLM website to help you get started with vLLM. Please visit vllm.ai to learn more. For events, please visit vllm.ai/events to join us.\nvLLM is a fast and easy-to-use library for LLM inference and serving.\nOriginally developed in the Sky Computing Lab at UC Berkeley, vLLM has grown into one of the most active open-source AI projects built and maintained by a diverse community of many dozens of academic institutions and companies from over 2000 contributors.\nvLLM is fast with:\n- State-of-the-art serving throughput\n- Efficient management of attention key and value memory with PagedAttention\n- Continuous batching of incoming requests, chunked prefill, prefix caching\n- Fast and flexible model execution with piecewise and full CUDA/HIP graphs\n- Quantization: FP8, MXFP8/MXFP4, NVFP4, INT8, INT4, GPTQ/AWQ, GGUF, compressed-tensors, ModelOpt, TorchAO, and more",
    "character_count": 958
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ac91700e-2845-47f1-b4a8-0e9f7a07c163",
    "technology": "vLLM",
    "technology_id": "source-vllm",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "vLLM",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm",
    "path": "vllm/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- Fast and flexible model execution with piecewise and full CUDA/HIP graphs\n- Quantization: FP8, MXFP8/MXFP4, NVFP4, INT8, INT4, GPTQ/AWQ, GGUF, compressed-tensors, ModelOpt, TorchAO, and more\n- Optimized attention kernels including FlashAttention, FlashInfer, TRTLLM-GEN, FlashMLA, and Triton\n- Optimized GEMM/MoE kernels for various precisions using CUTLASS, TRTLLM-GEN, CuTeDSL\n- Speculative decoding including n-gram, suffix, EAGLE, DFlash\n- Automatic kernel generation and graph-level transformations using torch.compile\n- Disaggregated prefill, decode, and encode\nvLLM is flexible and easy to use with:\n- Seamless integration with popular Hugging Face models\n- High-throughput serving with various decoding algorithms, including parallel sampling, beam search, and more\n- Tensor, pipeline, data, expert, and context parallelism for distributed inference\n- Streaming outputs\n- Generation of structured outputs using xgrammar or guidance\n- Tool calling and reasoning parsers",
    "character_count": 978
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e496b1bc-67b9-45a5-b804-983c1e0df9ea",
    "technology": "vLLM",
    "technology_id": "source-vllm",
    "category": "deployment",
    "organization": "vLLM",
    "license": "Apache-2.0",
    "priority": "high",
    "update_frequency": "weekly",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm",
    "path": "vllm/github_repository.json",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "- Streaming outputs\n- Generation of structured outputs using xgrammar or guidance\n- Tool calling and reasoning parsers\n- OpenAI-compatible API server, plus Anthropic Messages API and gRPC support\n- Efficient multi-LoRA support for dense and MoE layers\n- Support for NVIDIA GPUs, AMD GPUs, Intel GPUs, and x86/ARM/PowerPC CPUs. Additionally, diverse hardware plugins such as Google TPUs, Intel Gaudi, IBM Spyre, Huawei Ascend, Rebellions NPU, Apple Silicon, MetaX GPU, and more.\nvLLM seamlessly supports 200+ model architectures on Hugging Face, including:\n- Decoder-only LLMs (e.g., Llama, Qwen, Gemma)\n- Mixture-of-Expert LLMs (e.g., Mixtral, DeepSeek-V3, Qwen-MoE, GPT-OSS)\n- Hybrid attention and state-space models (e.g., Mamba, Qwen3.5)\n- Multi-modal models (e.g., LLaVA, Qwen-VL, Pixtral)\n- Embedding and retrieval models (e.g., E5-Mistral, GTE, ColBERT)\n- Reward and classification models (e.g., Qwen-Math)\nFind the full list of supported models here.",
    "character_count": 957
  },
  {
